yy
Diminished skin turgor 
yy
Altered consciousness, depending on the degree of dehydration
Investigations
yy
Detection of rotavirus antigen in stool by an enzyme immunoassay 
(EIA)
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
Correction of fluid and electrolyte deficits'),
('3','Constipation','1','Disorders of the Gastrointestinal Tract  ................................','19','19-21','yy Inability to move bowels yy Passing hard stools yy Infrequent passing of stools yy Straining to pass stools yy Feeling of incomplete evacuation of bowel yy Inability to pass flatus, colicky abdominal pain with or without vomiting','yy Frequent high pitched bowel sounds - suspect mechanical bowel obstruction yy Absent bowel sounds - suspect paralytic ileus yy Signs of peritonitis (generalised tenderness, guarding and rebound tenderness, refer appropriate section) -`suspect gangrenous bowel','yy Digital rectal examination (must be carried out in all patients with suspected diagnosis of constipation) yy Stool for occult blood yy Plain abdominal X-ray (erect and supine) yy Proctoscopy/proctosigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy (must not be done if acute intestinal obstruction is suspected)','Treatment objectives yy To identify possible cause of constipation yy To relieve constipation Non-pharmacological treatment yy Adherence to regular exercise yy High fibre diet yy Adequate fluid intake (minimum of 3.2L of water per day if no contraindications exist) — Constipation — Chapter 1: Disorders of the Gastrointestinal Tract 21 Pharmacological treatment A. Management of Constipation in Adults 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] yy Bisacodyl, oral, 10-20 mg at night Or yy Senna, oral, 15-30 mg at bedtime (maximum 70-100 mg daily). Doses above 70 mg should be divided 12 hourly Or yy Lactulose, oral, 15-30 ml daily until response, then 10-20 ml daily 2nd Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] yy Bisacodyl, rectal, 10 mg in the morning Or yy Glycerol suppositories, rectal, 4 g at night Or yy Liquid paraffin, oral, 10-30 ml at night Or yy Milk of Magnesia, oral, 5-10 ml in a glass of water, 12-24 hourly B. Management of Constipation in Children 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] yy Lactulose, oral, 10-18 years; 15 ml 12 hourly 5-10 years; 10 ml 12 hourly 1-5 years; 5 ml 12 hourly < 1 year; 2.5 ml 12 hourly Or yy Glycerol suppositories, rectal, 2-5 years; 2 g at night < 1 year; 1 g at night Or yy Bisacodyl, rectal, > 10 years; 5 mg in the morning < 10 years; on medical advice only Or yy Senna, oral, 6-12 years; 5-40 ml at bedtime 2-6 years; 2.5-20 ml at bedtime Note 1-5 Do not use magnesium salts in patients with impaired renal function.','yy Poor response to rehydration process (passing more stools than drinking) yy Poor drinking yy Blood in stool yy Poor feeding yy Altered consciousness/convulsions yy Diarrhoea and vomiting continuing for > 3 days Note 1-4 Prevention: Two (2) doses of Rotavirus vaccine, given at 6 - 10 weeks, is effective in prevent­ ing rotavirus diarrhoea. The 2nd dose should be given by 16 weeks, and not later than 24 weeks. Rotavirus vaccine is now currently given as part of routine immunization in Ghana. (See section on ‘Immunisation’). 10. Constipation In general, constipation refers to bowel movements that are infrequent or stools that are difficult to pass. There is great individual variation in normal bowel habits; therefore emphasis should be laid on changes in the bowel habit. Diarrhoea alternating with constipation may indicate a large bowel cancer especially in those aged 40 years and above. In children and the elderly, this may indicate chronic constipation with spurious diarrhoea. The habitual use of laxatives is very common in the community. This practice must be discouraged to avoid hypokalaemia and its consequences. Causes Medical yy Diet deficient in roughage yy Ignoring the urge to defaecate e.g. due to immobility yy Hypothyroidism yy Irritable bowel syndrome yy Hypercalcaemia yy Drugs e.g. atropine, codeine, morphine, tricyclic antidepressants,','— Constipation   —
