196
66.	 Seizures
A seizure is a clinical event caused by a transient disturbance of 
brain function due to an abnormal paroxysmal neuronal discharge in 
the brain. If these episodes are recurrent without an identifiable cause, 
they are commonly described as epilepsy.  The term status epilepticus 
is used for repeated seizures, which occur without the patient regaining 
consciousness between attacks. (See section on ‘Epilepsy and Status 
epilepticus’).
The clinical presentation depends on the part of the brain affected. 
Patients may sometimes describe the warning signals (termed a prodrome 
or aura), which they experienced before the event.  A detailed description 
by a witness is key. 
General anaesthesia and ventilation may be required in severe cases 
and where high doses of anticonvulsants are required.
Causes 
yy
Congenital, prenatal or perinatal injury
yy
Fevers, especially in children aged 6 months to 6 years (febrile 
convulsion)
yy
Cerebral malaria
yy
Infections e.g. meningitis, TB, HIV, abscesses in the brain
yy
Metabolic causes: hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, hyponatraemia, 
hyperosmolar diabetic state, uraemia, hepatic failure
yy
Idiopathic epilepsy  (See section on ‘Epilepsy’)
yy
Eclampsia
yy
Vascular diseases: hypertensive encephalopathy, stroke, myocardial 
infarction
yy
Space occupying lesions: tumour or malformations of the brain
yy
Head trauma
yy
Drugs and toxins: alcohol, antidepressants, metronidazole, drug and 
alcohol withdrawal
yy
Degenerative diseases e.g. dementia 
yy
Psychogenic:  (See section on ‘Psychogenic Seizures’)
Symptoms
yy
Loss of consciousness 
yy
Tongue biting 
yy
Foaming at the mouth
yy
Incontinence of stool and/or urine
yy
Aura (may include a strange gut feeling, somatosensory 
manifestations - visual, olfactory, gustatory or auditory e.g. strange 
smells/flashing lights)
yy
Muscle twitching and movements which may be focal or generalized
yy
After a seizure, the patient may be confused (post-ictal confusion) or 
may sleep for some time (post-ictal sleep)

