Referral Criteria 
Refer all cases for evaluation by psychologist or psychiatrist.
72.	 Insomnia
Insomnia is defined as a subjective report of difficulty with sleep 
initiation, duration, consolidation, or quality that occurs despite 
adequate opportunity for sleep, and that results in some form of daytime 
impairment. Insomnia symptoms occur quite commonly in the general 
population. Risk factors for insomnia include increasing age, female sex, 
co-morbid disorders such as medical, psychiatric, substance use, and shift 
work. Patients with psychiatric and chronic pain disorders have relatively 
high rates of insomnia. 
Causes 
yy
Behavioural - spending more time in bed in an effort to “catch up” 
on sleep
yy
Stress 
yy
Learned habits which do not enhance sleep
yy
Cognitive distortions (e.g. if one does not sleep throughout the night, 
one has not slept at all)
yy
Medicines (e.g.for treatment of common cold, hypertension, asthma)
yy
Caffeine-containing beverages (e.g. coffee, tea) 
yy
Withdrawal from alcohol and other drugs of abuse (e.g. cocaine, 
marijuana, amphetamines)
yy
Medical condition (e.g. sleep apnea, airway obstruction, liver 
disease, renal disease, thyroid disorders)
yy
Psychological and environmental factors 
yy
Other psychiatric disorders such as anxiety and mood disorders
yy
Travel (especially across time zones leading to jet lag) 
yy
Shift work
 
Symptoms
yy
Prolonged average time for falling asleep - longer than 30 minutes 
yy
Total sleep time less than 6.5 hours 
yy
Difficulty falling asleep
yy
Frequent awakenings
yy
Difficulty returning to sleep
yy
Awakening too early in the morning
yy
Sleep that does not feel restful, refreshing or restorative
yy
Anticipating poor sleep hours before bedtime, and becoming more 
alert and anxious as bedtime approaches
yy
Daytime effects of poor sleep:
yy
Fatigue and sleepiness
yy
Mood disturbances and cognitive difficulties
yy
Poor quality of life (worsened by interpersonal difficulties, or 

— Insomnia —
Chapter 10: Psychiatric Disorders
215
avoidance of day activities)
yy
Exacerbation of co-morbid conditions such as depression, high 
blood pressure, etc.  
yy
Day-to-day variability
Box 10-1: History taking and assessment of patients with Insomnia
yy
Characterization of the sleeping environment (couch/bed, light/dark, qui­
et/noisy, room temperature, alone/bed partner, TV on/off), patient’s state 
of mind (sleepy vs. wide awake, relaxed vs. anxious) 
yy
Identify perpetuating negative behaviours and cognitive processes 
yy
Assess Sleep-Wake Schedule with sleep dairy:  time to fall asleep (sleep la­
tency), number of awakenings, Wake time After Sleep Onset (WASO), sleep 
duration
yy
Assess  
yy
Breathing-related sleep disorders (snoring, gasping, coughing)
yy
Sleep related movement disorders (kicking, restlessness)
yy
Parasomnias (behaviors or vocalization)
yy
Co-morbid medical/neurological disorders (reflux, palpitations, seizures, 
headaches)
yy
Other physical sensations and emotions associated with wakefulness (such 
as pain, restlessness, anxiety, frustration, sadness) 
Assess Daytime Activities and Daytime Function: 
yy
Napping (frequency/day, times, voluntary/involuntary)
yy
Work (work times, work type such as driving or with dangerous conse­
quences, disabled, caretaker responsibilities)
yy
Lifestyle (sedentary/active, homebound, light exposure, exercise) 
yy
Travel (especially across time zones) 
yy
Quality of life and exacerbation of co-morbid disorders
Investigations
yy
FBC
yy
BUE and creatinine
yy
LFTs
yy
Blood glucose
yy
Thyroid function tests
Treatment 
Treatment objectives  
yy
Reduction of waking symptoms
yy
Improvement of daytime function
yy
Reduction of distress
yy
Treatment of co-morbid conditions
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Stimulus control therapy (avoid stimulating sleep environments such 
as leaving lights, TV and radio on) 
yy
Relaxation training
yy
Cognitive Behavior Therapy 
yy
Avoidance of day-time sleep
yy
Avoidance of excessive stimulant consumption pre-bed time (e.g.'),
