Box 10-2:  Screening of patients suspected of substance abuse
Physical appearance 
yy
Sweating, tremor, agitation, problem with coordination, gait. Rate these ap­
pearances and reassess them at regular intervals to monitor the progress 
of symptoms. If symptoms are increasing in severity, notify a senior staff 
member, or if available, a doctor
Suicide risk assessment 
yy
To determine the level of risk at a given time and to provide appropriate 
clinical care and management. Possible suicidal behaviour includes thinking 
about suicide, harming oneself or attempting suicide. 
yy
Screening questions of suicide risk: 
yy
Have things been so bad lately that you have thought you would rather 
not be here?
yy
Have you had any thoughts of harming yourself?
yy
Are you thinking of suicide?
yy
Do you have any plans to commit suicide?
yy
Have you ever tried to harm yourself?
yy
Have you made any current plans?
yy
Do you have access to any thing with which to hurt yourself? 
Mental state examination
yy
To determine: 
yy
The need for other psychological therapies
yy
Concomitant psychiatric conditions which place the patient or others at risk
yy
The patient’s capacity for informed consent and active participation in 
treatment planning
Assessment of psychosocial factors affecting withdrawal 
yy
Ask patient about:
yy
Reasons for presenting for withdrawal management at this time
yy
Past experiences, current knowledge and fears of withdrawal
yy
Perceived ability to cope with withdrawal and its treatment.
yy
Family supports and social networks available for withdrawal treatment:
yy
Potential barriers to successful withdrawal
yy
Care of children (assess possible neglect or physical or sexual abuse of chil­
dren or exposure to such harm from others and intervene to protect as 
soon as possible or refer to appropriate agency)
yy
Drug use of cohabitants
yy
Current legal issues
yy
Financial problems
yy
Work commitments
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To provide supportive care (information, stress reduction, 
reassurance)
yy
To teach coping skills (relaxation techniques, dietary guidelines, 
methods to reduce craving for the substance, sleep disturbance 
management)
yy
To manage difficult behavior (anxiety, agitation, panic and aggression)'),
