('98','Diabetes Mellitus','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','287','287-292','yy Usually none in many adults yy Polyuria and nocturia yy Polydipsia yy Unexplained weight loss yy Blurred vision yy Recurrent boils yy Recurrent pruritus vulvae yy Erectile dysfunction yy Symptoms related to chronic complications (e.g. ‘pins and needles’ sensation or numbness in the hands or feet, foot gangrene, poor vision etc.) yy Delivery of large babies (> 4 kg)','yy Usually none in most patients yy Lack of sensation in the feet or hands yy Foot gangrene yy Pedal oedema yy Impaired visual acuity yy Cataract yy Retinal changes on fundoscopy','Newly diagnosed patient yy Fasting or random blood glucose yy Oral glucose tolerance test (if required to confirm diagnosis) yy Blood urea, electrolytes and creatinine — Diabetes Mellitus — Chapter 12: Endocrine and Metabolic Disorders 289 yy Fasting blood lipid profile (adults) yy Glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) yy FBC yy ECG (adults) yy Urinalysis yy Urine ketones (with high initial glucose levels) During routine follow ups yy Blood glucose yy Recorded results of regular self-monitoring of fasting and ran­ dom tests at home by the patient using a glucose meter yy Periodic fasting or random tests during clinic reviews yy Glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) (at least twice a year) yy Blood lipid tests (annually, but more frequently if levels abnormal or on lipid lowering medication) yy Blood urea, electrolytes and creatinine (annually, but more frequently if levels abnormal) yy Urine microalbumin (annually)','Treatment objectives yy To relieve symptoms yy To prevent acute hyperglycaemic complications (i.e. ketoacidosis and the hyperosmolar state) yy To prevent treatment-related hypoglycaemia yy To achieve and maintain appropriate glycaemic targets yy Fasting blood glucose between 4-7 mmol/L (less intensive gly­ caemic targets in elderly patients) yy 2-hour post-meal blood glucose between 5-9 mmol/L (less in­ tensive glycaemic targets in elderly patients) yy Glycated haemoglobin 7 % or less (less intensive glycaemic tar­ gets in elderly patients) yy To ensure weight reduction in overweight and obese individuals yy To prevent chronic complications of diabetes by maintaining yy The glycaemic targets noted above yy Blood pressure less than 130/80 mmHg yy LDL-cholesterol less than 2.5 mmol/L — Diabetes Mellitus — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 290 Non-pharmacological treatment Box 12-1: Therapeutic lifestyle modification Diet: yy Complex carbohydrates (e.g. kenkey, yam, plantain etc.) are preferred yy Avoid refined sugars as in soft drinks, or adding sugar to beverages. Artifi­ cial sweeteners and ‘diet’ soft drinks, which do not contain glucose, may however be used yy A day’s diet must generally consist of; yy Carbohydrates (60%), protein (15%) and fat (20%) mostly of plant-origin and low in animal fat yy Salt (sodium chloride) < 5 g/day yy Fruits and vegetables ≥ 400 g/day yy Soluble fibre (e.g. as found in oats, beans, apples, nuts etc.) yy A reduced total caloric content (portions) of food and an increase in the amount of fibre e.g. vegetables, fruits and cereals yy Owing to the special needs of children for a diet that will ensure optimal growth, their diet must be determined by a dietician in consultation with a paediatrician Alcohol: yy Intake of alcohol is prohibited for individuals less than 18 years of age. While low consumption is permissible in adult patients, moderate to heavy drinking of alcohol increases the total caloric intake and may worsen over­ weight and obesity. It may also increase the risk of hypoglycaemia Exercise: yy Regular, simple exercise e.g. 30 minutes brisk walking at least 3 days a week in ambulant patients yy All advice on exercise must give consideration to the patient’s age and the presence of complications and other medical conditions Pharmacological treatment A. Type 1 diabetes patients and Type 2 patients on insulin mono