 pressure below 140/90mmHg may cause foetal distress and should be avoided. BP monitoring must be carried out every 15-30 minutes until the BP is reduced and the patient is stable. Thereafter monitoring can be done by 2-4 hourly. Daily weighing of the patient is essential. Symptoms yy Frontal headaches yy Vomiting yy Visual disturbances such as double vision (diplopia), blurred vision, flashes of light yy Epigastric pain yy Decrease in urine production (oliguria) Signs yy Elevated blood pressure yy Liver tenderness yy Urine production of < 30 ml/hour or < 400 ml/24 hours yy Increased tendon reflexes yy Presence of ankle clonus (occasionally) Investigations yy FBC yy Blood clotting profile (bedside clotting time, prothrombin time, INR, APTT) yy Serum uric acid yy BUE and Creatinine yy Urinalysis and culture yy Liver function tests yy Random blood glucose yy Daily assessment of urine proteins yy Ultrasound scan for close foetal growth monitoring Treatment Treatment objectives yy To reduce the blood pressure, but not lower than 140/90 mmHg yy To prevent the mother from suffering from complications of the hypertension such as a stroke yy To prevent fits/eclampsia yy To stabilise the patient and deliver her if eclampsia is imminent Non-pharmacological treatment yy Early delivery of mother if eclampsia is imminent yy If the patient is not symptomatic and the pregnancy is less than 34 weeks allow pregnancy to continue if the foetal condition would allow yy If the pregnancy is 34 weeks or more consider delivery after stabilisation',NULL,'— Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia —
