clinically indicated 
(See section on ‘Shock’)
D.     Anaesthesia for manual removal of placenta
yy
Morphine, IV, 2.5 - 5 mg stat. as required (if no anaesthetist is avail­
able)
Or
yy
Pethidine, IV slowly or IM, 1 mg/kg 6 - 8 hourly as required (max. 100 
mg per dose) 
And 
yy
Diazepam, slow IV, 5-10 mg 8 hourly as required (NOT more than 2.5 
mg per minute) 
Note 13-8
Do not mix pethidine and diazepam in the same syringe. Monitor respiratory 
rate of patient closely. Stop drugs if respiratory rate is less than 12 per minute.
Or
yy
Ketamine, IM, 5-10 mg/kg stat.
Or 
yy
Ketamine, IV, 0.5-2 mg/kg stat.
Note 13-9
Ketamine must be used only by trained Medical Officer/anaesthetist
Provide antibiotics prophylaxis after manual removal of placenta or exploration 
of uterus or repair of birth canal tears.
E.     Secondary Postpartum Haemorrhage
yy
Oxytocin, IV infusion, 20 units in 1 L of Normal Saline
Or
yy
Ergometrine, oral, 500 microgram 8 hourly for 3 days
