('181','HIV Post Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) for exposed healthcare personel','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','464','464-467','yy None Box 17-1: Steps to prevent occupational transmission of HIV In the event of possible exposure to HIV the following actions should be taken: yy The wound site should be cleaned with soap and water yy For mucous membranes, the exposed area should be flushed with plenty of water (e.g. eyes with water or saline) yy Assess the level of risk: the risk of possible infection from the exposure should be assessed and classified based on the categories below: yy Very Low Exposure: yy Exposure of potentially infectious material to intact skin) yy Low Risk Exposure: yy Exposure to a small volume of blood or body fluids contaminated with blood from asymptomatic HIV-positive patient yy Injury with a solid needle or any superficial injury or mucocutaneous ex­ posure yy High Risk Exposure: yy Exposure to a large volume of blood or potentially infectious fluids yy Exposure to blood or body fluids contaminated with blood from an HIV positive patient with high viral load yy Injury with a hollow bore needle/deep and extensive injury from a con­ taminated sharp instrument yy Exposure to blood from an HIV drug resistant patient','yy None Symptoms yy None Box 17-1: Steps to prevent occupational transmission of HIV In the event of possible exposure to HIV the following actions should be taken: yy The wound site should be cleaned with soap and water yy For mucous membranes, the exposed area should be flushed with plenty of water (e.g. eyes with water or saline) yy Assess the level of risk: the risk of possible infection from the exposure should be assessed and classified based on the categories below: yy Very Low Exposure: yy Exposure of potentially infectious material to intact skin) yy Low Risk Exposure: yy Exposure to a small volume of blood or body fluids contaminated with blood from asymptomatic HIV-positiv