('251','Burns','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','624','624-629','yy Pain (very severe in superficial type and less in deeper burns) yy Swelling yy Difficulty in breathing yy Blisters — Burns — Chapter 23: Trauma And Injuries 625','yy Shock yy Inhalational injury (burnt nasal hairs, soot in the throat, hoarseness of the voice and black particles in sputum) yy Swelling yy Blister formation yy Charring of tissue (deep burns) yy ECG changes (electrical burns)','yy FBC and sickling yy BUE and Creatinine yy Wound swab yy Blood gases yy Chest X-ray','Treatment objectives yy Prevent further injury from burns yy Relieve pain yy Replace lost fluid yy Prevent infection of burn wound yy Aid healing of the burn wound yy Avoid complications Non-pharmacological treatment yy Remove clothing from affected part yy Put affected part under running water if available till pain goes away or it is reduced yy If chemical is in powder form brush it off the affected part and put under running water yy Secure airway yy Leave intact blisters alone yy Do not apply any creams or ointment yy Affected limbs should be elevated yy Good nutrition yy Psychological therapy yy Physiotherapy yy Reassure patient Pharmacological treatment A. For burns with Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) of less than 10% in children and 15% in adults Note 23-6 yy Update tetanus prophylaxis yy (See section on ‘Tetanus prophylaxis’) — Burns — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 626 yy Liberal oral fluids yy Dress burns with Silver Sulphadiazine yy Oral Analgesia yy Paracetamol, oral, Adults 500 mg-1 g 6-8 hourly Children 6-12 years; 250-500 mg 6-8 hourly 1-5 years; 120-250 mg 6-8 hourly 3 months-1 year; 60-120 mg 6-8 hourly Or yy Naproxen EC, oral, Adults 250-500 mg 12 hourly as required Children Not indicated Or yy Celecoxib, oral, Adults 400 mg stat. Then 200 mg 12-24 hourly as required Children Not indicated Or yy Diclofenac, oral, Adults 50 mg 8 hourly or 100 mg