253.	Bites and Stings 
SNAKE BITE
Most snake bites are non-poisonous. Vipers are the commonest 
cause of poisonous snake bites in tropical Africa. Others are the cobras and 
water snakes. All cases of snake bites (venomous/non-venomous) should 
be observed for at least 6 hours. Identify the type of snake if possible. 
Don’t rely too much on fang marks; however multiple fang marks usually 
indicate a non-poisonous bite whereas one or two fang marks suggest a 
poisonous bite. It is important to determine whether envenomation has 
occurred. The role of tourniquets and incision over the site of the bite are 
controversial issues and are to be avoided.
Causes 
yy
Snakes
Symptoms
yy
Pain 
yy
Bleeding
yy
Swelling

— Bites and Stings  —
Chapter 23: Trauma And Injuries
633
yy
Fainting
yy
Dark-coloured urine
yy
Headache
yy
Muscle ache
yy
Fear
yy
Loss of consciousness
Signs
(Poisonous snake bites) 
Cardiovascular:
yy
Hypotension, shock, cardiac arrhythmias
yy
Spontaneous systemic bleeding, from bite site, mucosa and old 
wounds, haematuria
yy
Dark urine from myoglobinuria and intravascular haemolysis  
Neuromuscular:
yy
Cranial nerve paralysis - ptosis, opthalmoplegia, slurred speech
yy
Bulbar respiratory paralysis - drooling, and inability to breath 
properly
yy
Impaired consciousness, seizures
yy
Meningism 
yy
Tender and stiff muscles
Local effects:
yy
Rapid progression of swelling to more than half of bitten limb
yy
Blistering, necrosis and bruising
yy
Fascial compartmentalisation on bitten digits
Investigations
yy
Full blood count
yy
Renal function test
yy
20 minutes whole blood clotting test (leave 2-5 ml of blood in dried 
test tube. Failure to clot after 20 minutes implies incoagulable blood)
yy
Liver function test
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To relieve pain and anxiety
yy
To support the respiration or circulation if indicated
yy
To counteract the spread and effect of the snake venom
yy
To prevent secondary infection
Non-pharmacological treatment
First Aid  
yy
Immobilization/splinting of the affected limb. Do not move the limb 
that has been bitten - the more it is moved, the faster the poison 
spreads.  Carry the person on a stretcher and tie the limb to a straight 
piece of wood. If ice is available, wrap pieces in cloth and place it 

